{"id":8200577,"docketId":65298385,"caseName":"Chronicle & Gazette Publishing Co. v. Attorney General","caseNameFull":"Chronicle & Gazette Publishing Co., Inc. v. Attorney General of New Hampshire","slug":"chronicle-gazette-publishing-co-v-attorney-general","docketNumber":"No. 780","dateFiled":"1947-01-06","dateArgued":null,"dateCertGranted":null,"year":1947,"scdbId":"","direction":"","votesMajority":null,"votesMinority":null,"disposition":"","posture":"","syllabus":null,"summary":null,"headnotes":null,"proceduralHistory":null,"attorneys":"Stanley M. Burns and Elisha Hanson for appellant., Robert W. Upton for appellees.","natureOfSuit":"","citationCount":0,"judges":"","jurisdictionType":"","opinions":[{"id":8162513,"type":"majority","typeCode":"020lead","perCuriam":false,"author":"","joinedBy":"","pageCount":null,"textLength":1378,"isHtml":true,"text":"<opinion type=\"majority\">\n<author id=\"Ah8r\">\n<em>Per Curiam:</em>\n</author>\n<p id=\"AJI\">The motion to dismiss is granted, and the appeal is dismissed for the want of a substantial federal question. (1) <em>Nebbia </em>v. <em>New York, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"102224\"><a href=\"/opinion/102224/nebbia-v-new-york/\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Nebbia v. New York\">291 U. S. 502</a></span>; <em>Olsen </em>v. <em>Nebraska, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"103522\"><a href=\"/opinion/103522/olsen-v-nebraska-ex-rel-western-reference-bond-assn-inc/\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Olsen v. Nebraska Ex Rel. Western Reference &amp; Bond Assn.,...\">313 U. S. 236</a></span>. (2) <em>Associated Press </em>v. <em>Labor Board, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"9841931\"><a href=\"/opinion/102807/associated-press-v-national-labor-relations-board/\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Associated Press v. National Labor Relations Board\">301 U. S. 103</a></span>; <em>Associated Press </em>v. <em>United States, </em><span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"9419684\"><a href=\"/opinion/104178/associated-press-v-united-states/\" aria-description=\"Citation for case: Associated Press v. United States\">326 U. S. 1</a></span>. Mr. Justice Douglas, Mr. Justice Murphy, and Mr. Justice Rutledge are of the opinion that probable jurisdiction should be noted.</p>\n</opinion>"}],"citations":[]}